"Let $T$ be a tree with $\\ell$ leaves, let $\\mathcal{T}$ denote the family of trees used in the discrepancy definition, and let $\\mathcal{D}^1(T,\\mathcal{T})$ denote the one-dimensional, or complex, discrepancy. Write $\\Delta(T)$ for the maximum degree of $T$. **Complex discrepancy conjecture.** For every tree $T$ with $\\ell$ leaves,\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D}^1(T,\\mathcal{T})\\ge \\frac{1}{2\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2\\ell}\\right)}=\\left(1+o(1)\\right)\\frac{\\ell}{\\pi}.\n$$\n\nIf, in addition, $\\Delta(T)=\\omega(1)$, then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D}^1(T,\\mathcal{T})=\\left(1+o(1)\\right)\\frac{\\ell}{\\pi}.\n$$\n\nThe preceding theorem proves the weaker universal lower bound $\\mathcal{D}^1(T,\\mathcal{T})\\ge \\ell/\\pi$; the conjecture proposes the sharp trigonometric bound and asymptotic equality for trees of unbounded maximum degree."